{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli Wikipedia Summary**\n\n**Early life and reign**\n\nRaymond III of Tripoli was born circa 1140 as the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 at around twelve years old after his father was assassinated. His minority led to a regency by his mother Hodierna until he came of age. Raymond III faced challenges from internal rivals like the Embriaco family and external threats from Nur ad-Din the Zengid emir. He sought support from Jerusalem securing the regency of Baldwin III and later Amalric I.\n\n***highlighted section part 1***\n\n**Conflict and captivity**\n\nRaymond III played a key role in the politics of the Crusader states. He supported Amalric I against the Byzantine Empire in 1171. In 1174 he allied with the Assassins against Nur ad-Din but was captured in 1175 by the Assassins during a meeting. He was imprisoned until 1177 when William of Montferrat negotiated his release. This captivity weakened his position in Tripoli. Upon his return he struggled to maintain control over the county amidst growing instability.\n\n***highlighted section part 2***\n\n**Leadership and later years**\n\nIn 1174 Raymond III became regent for the leper king Baldwin IV of Jerusalem. He served as a key advisor to the young king and played a crucial role in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where he helped a crusader victory against Saladin. However internal strife in Jerusalem between factions including the Ibelins and Courtenays weakened his influence. He briefly supported Raymond of Saint-Gilles against Guy of Lusignan but later reconciled with Guy. Raymond III died in 1187 just before Saladins conquest of Jerusalem. His death marked the end of an era for the Crusader states.\n\n**Legacy**\n\nRaymond III is remembered as a pragmatic leader who navigated the complex politics of the Crusader states. His regency for Baldwin IV was marked by efforts to unite factions against Saladin. Despite setbacks like his captivity and the loss of Jerusalem he remained a respected figure until his death. His life reflects the broader struggles of the Crusader states in the late 12th century.\n\n**_highlighted section part 3_**", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.17142000794410706}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.4044681787490845}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.353653589030728e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.020910173654556274}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00012230125139467418}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": " Wikipedia", "logprob": -4.78422212600708}, {"token": " Summary", "logprob": -0.008111155591905117}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.00020394629973452538}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -1.4612456560134888}, {"token": "Early", "logprob": -0.22097396850585938}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -1.055749535560608}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.21790573000907898}, {"token": " reign", "logprob": -0.9905304312705994}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.8894219994544983}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.002845288719981909}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0013217531377449632}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.7582123279571533}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.011383228935301304}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.06259196996688843}, {"token": " born", "logprob": -0.08185949176549911}, {"token": " circa", "logprob": -4.797104358673096}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00016008525562938303}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.2411095415009186e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.0004120216181036085}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.001401276676915586}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -0.06274905800819397}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.2949238121509552}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.003158820327371359}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -